bound-lock/1 about="# Exact release artifact\n\nPacking is a host operation. An archive digest names those archive bytes; it is not the identity of a Topos standing. The release pipeline makes one archive and transfers that same file to its destinations. Repeating a pack operation is not a proof of identical bytes.\n\nThe archive conformance vector declares the host package command, digest algorithm, execution deadline, response bound and its sample packages. It packs each sample once, hashes the resulting bytes, verifies both destination copies, installs offline without package scripts and compares every listed installed member with the input bytes. It executes the installed entry and checks its declared output. Changed archive bytes refuse by digest before they can be treated as the named artifact. The negative control fails when that verification is removed.\n\nThe samples use independent packages with different contents. Their outputs and member digests are captured by the executable vector; their file presence alone is not evidence. The selected publication reader measures this quartet through the existing process contract, and native receipts supply its member status to the plan.\n\nThis is conformance of the packaging recipe. Acceptance of a particular Bound candidate additionally requires the exact archive audit, clean installation and product CLI captures for that archive. A previous archive's captures cannot certify changed implementation bytes. The final candidate must be rebuilt after admitted metadata changes, with every changed member accounted for. The product witness uses the installed reader directly, without a checkout ledger or source conditions.\n\nRendering and installation are different boundaries. README, the manifest and the standard MIT text remain native renders. Publication to a registry or forge is a later owner action; this vector does not publish, authenticate to an account, create a tag or infer an external release from a local file.\n" at=witness:public-contracts-by-operation by=owner epoch=1 form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-archive shape=docs/contracts/archive.md sig="ed25519:era1:7MpRdUGbJLNAt2bVYph0Ccabt0h63G1XnKoK2rTV8535fugiRCLU2AuTlt9ItSFLc1P6aDCkGPfcsFNXS8ESBg==" value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# fold\n\n`bound fold` reads the admitted place. Configuration output names readings and says no encounters computed; its counts are not independent-origin states. A declared cells view projects admitted typed history into Obligations. A selected world defines other views; its contract decides when empty output is valid. A refusal or nonzero status wins over any parseable diagnostic stdout. `--check` verifies receipts without inventing a missing reading. The published evidence vector folds fresh places, preserves admitted key attribution, reads a native receipt and checks exact withdrawals. The typed-object quartet covers object states separately.\n\n# land\n\n`bound land <lot>` admits authenticated lines through the same boundary used by signing and previews. Authority, coverage, historical wire and typed context remain distinct checks. Unknown wire and tampered signatures refuse. Exact redelivery returns ONCE; it does not append another origin or grant new authority. Withdrawals remain exact under their declared contract. A refusal does not append a partial act. The published evidence vector lands fresh places, refuses unauthorized writes and tampering, redelivers signed bytes and exercises exact withdrawals. The signing-port vector additionally checks interrupted or invalid command responses do not land a partial lot.\n\n# sign\n\n`bound sign <lot>` returns canonical signed lines after validating the whole lot. The admitted key selects a declared signer; `--signer` selects the same port explicitly. File and command mechanisms use one port. Topos signing-lot/1 frames canonical bytes in lot order, one invocation per lot, and returns signatures and public-key IDs. Verification checks the admitted public key, independently of the mechanism. A command is invoked without a shell and under declared bounds. No secret output is disclosed in refusals. Missing, invalid, partial, timed-out or failed responses refuse; `land` cannot commit their partial output. The sample compares file and command signatures and landed lines, checks invocation count and ledger hashes, and refuses an unknown wire version. The sample uses ephemeral keys outside the place; no owner key is included.\n\n# Committed act results\n\nThe opt-in `local-act@1` contract is selected by `wire/act-result`, `wire/act-result-scope` and `wire/act-result-context`. An admitted `view/act measure=id value=act` makes `bound fold --as act <identity>` available. Missing or conflicting declarations refuse; an absent profile preserves the historical interface. Topos defines the canonical record and result contract.\n\nAdmission publishes the original signed records, their logical placements and one native result receipt atomically. A failure before commit writes no partial act. The result names the committed digest and local epoch; replay returns the original result without another epoch or origin. A post-commit error is not a rollback. Read the committed result before deciding whether to retry.\n\nThe query verifies the record signatures and coverage against the authority snapshot at admission, and the native receipt against its retained provenance. Later key retirement or profile replacement cannot authorize a new act but does not erase a verifiable historical result. Admission is not conformance: the output says `admitted · conformance not asserted`. Missing or corrupt results refuse by name. Host paths and delivery counts are not identity inputs.\n\n## Release approval\n\n`fold --as release <approval-file>` verifies the external approval against the place authorizer, its admitted public key, coverage and signature era. Exactly one signed release/<tag> digest, release/artifact name and sources/archive URL are required. It prints `READY sha256:<digest> <name> <source>` or refuses with the coordinate and cause. This is an approval judgment, distinct from the place release-policy verdict. It never fetches, executes, signs or lands.\n\n## Standing admission\n\nA uses/ selection admits canonical standing bytes, never an archive. The bytes must already be held under their verified digest or in the single local coordinate explicitly named by needs. Admission checks identity and canonical form without fetching, executing or writing the content store. Missing or corrupt bytes refuse the whole lot. A corrupt held copy cannot be hidden by another source. Archives remain separate dependency declarations.\n\n\n## Declared reading identity\n\nA place selects both `wire/reading-context value=reading-context@1` and `wire/reading-receipt value=reading-receipt@1`. Partial or conflicting selections refuse. Every reader under this profile declares an input projection; a legacy reader is refused by name instead of receiving a new identity without its closure.\n\nThe host commits the full instrument reference, the verified capsule implementation, effective reader policy, declared transitive references, each input's actual bytes and dependent receipt identities. The process environment is captured explicitly: inherited variable values (hashed, never printed), the place working directory, child flags, runtime bytes and platform conditions. The same captured boundary is handed to execution and keys the capsule cache. These references grant neither authority nor independence and cannot prove undeclared inputs absent.\n\nAn unchanged cross-process fold reads retained receipts with zero opened readings. Changed input reopens that reading and its dependents; changing shared implementation, policy or execution conditions invalidates the readings that depend on them. The semantic receipt names the reading and result together; elapsed time and signature envelopes remain separate. Old receipts are not promoted.\n\nRetained evidence may be inspected without asserting a current response. Reusing a current response also requires the existing snapshot-access contract: matching principal, authorization, request, implementation and snapshot, declared freshness, and both inspection and reuse permission. Unknown or expired freshness, withdrawn permission or changed context refuse before running a provider. A new observation needs its own admitted act.\n\n`fold --check` verifies the retained published evidence and instrument. It opens no reader and grants no permission to assert an old response today. A live fold additionally compares the current captured execution boundary.\n" at=witness:complete-reading-context-with-native-reuse by=owner epoch=1 form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-cli shape=docs/contracts/cli.md sig="ed25519:era1:hWNn6aQGNNXRE0YS7tFdBjyUUcMgu/qBhA74KSfaGoMyTSHMqW0Va+Och6fNRO++gyxP9dyCv536SNoBkw1eCA==" value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Explanation — historical profile\n\nThe declared view selects idea, what-holds and refuses from the byte-pinned historical document provider. The place owns its language and prose. Idea renders its own heading and body. The other two regions require their declared law readings or captured refusals; absent evidence supplies no invented result. The executable sample uses two unrelated places, checks the complete explanation file, repeats the product fold without a file difference and asks the named view directly. It also refuses a well-formed view asking an unoffered region. This quartet proves own-prose projection and absence under this profile; it does not certify law readings, a rejection corpus, typed-object states or the pending general document contract. Bound does not parse this prose.\n\n# README — historical profile\n\nThe declared view selects hero@3, commands@3 and idea@3 from the byte-pinned historical document provider. The place supplies its name, tagline, language, sentence rule and idea. Commands and measured badges are absent in the sample: no command, number or success is invented to fill them. Two unrelated places check the generated README, a second unchanged product fold and the named view; a declared unoffered region refuses by name. The implementation member is verified against the admitted historical artifact. This quartet proves the selected prose profile, not command execution, badges with measured values or a general document grammar. The eight text@3 demands remain unpaid.\n\n# Citation file — historical profile\n\nThe declared view asks the pinned document provider for cff at resolution 3. It projects the place name, version and optional citation metadata into CFF text. The sample uses the exact implementation member from the admitted historical artifact, names its hash and license, and exercises two unrelated places through the compiled CLI. It checks complete citation bytes and two equal readings; title and version follow each place. An unoffered region refuses by view name. Missing optional metadata is omitted; missing name can produce an empty answer under this historical profile. Rendering is not verification of a scientific claim or an external DOI. This pinned profile does not implement the pending general document contract.\n\n# Guide — historical profile\n\nThe declared view asks seq from the pinned historical document provider. The place owns page/seq lines and their heading. Canonical input order supplies the steps; the provider numbers that order and writes the requested shape. The executable sample uses two unrelated procedures, checks their complete Markdown and reads each view twice without differences. A declared view with an unoffered region refuses by name. An empty step set may have an empty answer; it is not invented evidence. A rendered instruction does not prove that instruction was executed. The implementation bytes remain the existing pinned profile, not a document grammar inside Bound.\n\n# Reference — historical profile\n\nThe declared view combines term, set and ref from the pinned historical document provider. The profile projects the place term and meaning, its selected world names and links to its declared Markdown shapes. The executable sample runs two unrelated places, checks their own term and meaning, the actual selected world and relative guide link, and captures two identical CLI readings. A view asking an unoffered region refuses by name. Missing terms or links can yield empty component answers; these are not measurements or a claim that obligations were paid. These historical regions and selectors belong to the selected Topos; Bound supplies verified inputs and dispatch only. The general document contract remains unpaid.\n\n# Manifest render profile\n\nThis declared view uses the selected verbatim-prose provider at text@3. The place assigns its complete manifest text to its output shape; Bound verifies and dispatches the selected offer and writes that shape. The provider does not infer a package schema, dependency graph or fields from the filename. The sample renders two places with distinct JSON bodies twice and checks exact file bytes. Two distinct prose bodies assigned to the same shape refuse; earlier outputs remain unchanged. This proves rendering, not that arbitrary JSON is an installable package.\n\n# Workflow render profile\n\nThis declared view uses the selected verbatim-prose provider at text@3. The place owns the workflow text and its shape; Bound does not infer a forge or manufacture pipeline steps. The sample writes distinct YAML bodies in two places, reads the rendered files twice and compares exact bytes. Conflicting bodies for one shape refuse without replacing prior outputs. This is a render contract, not evidence that a forge executed, staged or published a release. The release pipeline has its separate artifact gate.\n\n# Usage render profile\n\nThis declared view uses the selected verbatim-prose provider at text@3. A prose line with measure=text and the requested shape supplies its own body. The sample renders distinct Markdown bodies in two places twice and compares exact bytes. Multiple identical bodies are one text; differing bodies for one shape refuse. It invents no sections or documentation schema. This existing profile does not pay the eight general-document demands or validate commands that prose happens to mention; executable commands require their own captured vectors.\n\n# Build configuration — historical profile\n\nThe declared tsconfig view asks the admitted package provider for its build region. It projects the place's build/* lines whose shape matches the requested file: true and false become JSON booleans, other option values remain strings, and include separates its declared alternatives. The byte-fixed fixture names the implementation member and its artifact independently; Bound does not interpret compiler options. Two unrelated places exercise the compiled CLI, check the complete generated configuration and two byte-identical readings. A declared view asking an unoffered region refuses by name. This profile renders configuration; it does not validate compiler options or claim a successful build. An empty option set renders an empty compilerOptions object. It is a pinned historical profile, not the pending general document contract.\n" at=witness:public-contracts-by-operation by=owner epoch=1 form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-documents shape=docs/contracts/documents.md sig="ed25519:era1:E9jLJ8Bz44TL5mV5Ch85u46RMkcObtiYOE6HAnsymdvKpcoWVLEHXCfwYYXVZxsEWF9ngCx4qtzKAxfVAhSjBA==" value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Pure object projection\n\n`@lapxo/bound/engine` exports `objectFold` and the existing `ObjectReading`, `ObjectRecord` and `ObjectContext` types. The CLI and receipt paths use that same implementation. Its Obligations 0.3.5 algebra is unchanged.\n\nThe input is authenticated and context-admitted effective history with selected deterministic forms and parameters. This function does not admit records, verify signatures, resolve content, run commands, select clocks or write storage. Applications must perform preparation before projection.\n\nWithin each epoch, a cell declaration precedes its uses in effective order. A same-epoch act before its cell refuses in preparation by name: `object context: cell not declared before <act-id>`. Lower-epoch cell declarations remain eligible even when their envelope arrives later. Effective sign/join order is preserved; transport arrival does not authorize arbitrary semantic reordering. The consumed Topos contract must be published before this export is admitted for release.\n\nOutputs preserve existing attributed origins, local and travelling marks, exact withdrawals, floor and ceiling parts and REQUIRED/FREE/FORBIDDEN/CONFLICT decisions. Raw object JSON is not a new canonical identity. Byte identity requires the existing declared reading projection.\n\nMalformed or unauthenticated records are outside this pure projector's input contract. Refusal, authority and interrupted-admission vectors exercise the host boundary; they do not claim cryptographic admission inside WASM. No host callback calculates an algebraic result in a WASM comparison.\n\n## Public conformance samples\n\n`samples/engine-interval` and `samples/engine-alphabet` are formed places: TARGET.bound, receipts.bound, package.json, the declared reader.js and inputs/sample.json. The instrument exercises the same projector against prepared fixtures; its reader is not a command users invoke. `fold <sample-directory> --check` verifies each portable receipt. Cold native `fold --as receipts` recomputes the declared conformance reading without a private store.\n\n`samples/no/engine-interval.json` and `samples/no/engine-alphabet.json` are negative data fixtures. Their captured native preparation refusal is `cell not declared before s-B`; no closed receipts are asserted for refusing input. The actual CLI vector is `vectors/engine-object.cli.json`. This conformance reader does not authenticate arbitrary signatures, coverage or clocks.\n\n\n## Optional WASM host adapter\n\nThe optional `@lapxo/bound/wasm` entry evaluates the existing pure projection in QuickJS WASM. It performs no admission, signature verification, state calculation of its own, transport, key discovery or effects. Host authentication must finish before records become projection inputs. A valid digest identifies bytes; it grants no authority or confinement guarantee.\n\nThe host supplies the trusted runtime/WASM/module digests, exact byte lengths, entry, aliases and limits. These expectations must come from its accepted configuration, independently of fetched place bytes. Closed request metadata and exact lengths are checked before copying, hashing or worker handoff. Shared assets refuse. Every supplied byte is verified and copied before execution; the closed runtime bundle has no imports and receives the already verified WASM binary. Guest imports resolve only within the verified module map. No second fetch occurs.\n\n`runWasmRequest` returns declared text, without inventing a JSON canonicalization or number policy. The caller's declared entry chooses the serialization contract. Memory and stack limits govern the guest; they do not describe total host/WASM initialization memory. Guest memory, stack and execution budgets are explicit host inputs. Initialization and execution times are separate readings, not a speed guarantee.\n\n`evaluateWasm` runs in a host worker. It accepts an AbortSignal and an explicit worker lifetime. Cancellation, timeout, malformed messages, failed serialization and worker errors terminate the worker and return no successful result. Guest state remains provisional until success; no host write port is supplied. A terminated computation produces no successful receipt. The direct `runWasmRequest` entry has deadline interruption but cannot asynchronously receive an external abort while synchronously executing; callers needing that contract use `evaluateWasm`.\n\n\n### Native optional WASM projection\n\nThe existing object view consumes an optional, authenticated live declaration `engine/object form=alphabet measure=id value=wasm@1`. With no selector it retains the ESM projection; a conflicting or unknown selector refuses. This profile selects only the explicit object view, not every other view of the place. The host admits and folds configuration with the existing authority and withdrawal rules before selecting execution.\n\nThe place declares five interval limits: `engine/wasm/memory`, `engine/wasm/stack` and `engine/wasm/response` in bytes; `engine/wasm/execution` and `engine/wasm/worker-lifetime` in milliseconds. Missing, ambiguous or invalid limits refuse; there is no inferred fallback. The installed instrument binds the guest module and supported form standings to their context implementation artifacts. A place cannot choose another verifier or executable through this selection; an unsupported form or different implementation refuses.\n\nContext initialization is measured before place execution. If observed initialized guest memory exceeds the declared memory budget, the operation refuses without running place code. Host initialization memory is outside that guest execution limit; the worker lifetime bounds the entire attempt. During execution the unchanged declared memory, stack and execution limits apply. SIGINT reaches the worker cancellation port; cancellation or failure emits no successful object result.\n\nFor the opt-in `wasm@1` profile, the host verifies the selected context implementation before importing it. Every SHA-256 member declared by the context offer’s `restsOn` is checked by digest, and the existing `moduleClosure` validates the imported closure. Missing, altered, undeclared or unresolved imported members refuse before context code executes. This establishes content identity and import closure, not correctness, authority or sandbox confinement. Admission still precedes projection, and this check grants no permission to reuse a retained answer as current.\n" at=witness:bound020-native-closure by=owner epoch=1 form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-engine shape=docs/contracts/engine.md sig="ed25519:era1:GEGr5xYyJ0PKRbtfumx9UlxhBKToO9g0+pAntpHzU5AvD6ipq9Y2qjffwyWpASdtqiQhDLE7ntTrDtObXQNIDg==" value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Authenticated evidence view\n\nThe declared evidence@8 view projects authenticated delivery history of this place. It preserves signatures and key attribution, including admitted receipts from an attest-class key. Evidence is not normative configuration, proof of truth, or an independent observation origin. Authority and coverage are checked by the existing Topos contract; no consumer or device type changes that boundary.\n\n\nThe no sample specifies an uncovered write and tampered signed bytes. Both must refuse by name. Exact withdrawals remove only covered normative lines; authenticated evidence remains inspectable. The vectors assert status, signature verification and observable CLI output. No private key bytes are printed or shipped.\n\n## Folded standing\n\nA place declares `view/standing role=demands form=alphabet measure=id value=standing`. `fold --as standing` authenticates each local line against its admission snapshot, then applies revisions and covered exact withdrawals through the existing fold. Every declared key class participates, including attest. Committed acts and historical journals have the same public output. Retiring a key does not erase its previously admitted evidence. Replay adds nothing. Foreign history grants no local authority. This projection is distinct from authenticated delivery history and does not create a new authority lock. An undeclared standing view refuses by name.\n" at=witness:bound-017-public-boundary by=owner epoch=1 form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-evidence-view shape=docs/contracts/evidence-view.md sig="ed25519:era1:DeAxkLXbGl2Onpt8emtAVvZCffWksrrZS++Z3TtNLQ8HTtCacrULO87qMPftThKyy6OJiNDpM6OtCguxjzP2Ag==" value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Public host ports\n\n`/engine` and `/reference` export `readContent`, `retainContent` and `invokeSigningPort`, with `ContentReadPort`, `PersistencePort`, `SigningPort`, `DigestPort` and `PortLimits`. There is no default network, store, account or signer. `objectFold` remains pure and never calls a port.\n\nEvery operation takes an explicit positive safe-integer `timeoutMs` and `maxBytes`, and an optional AbortSignal. Invalid limits and pre-cancellation refuse before the callback. Acquisition, digest calculation and retention share the operation lifetime. A timeout aborts the supplied signal and returns no successful result. This is a cooperative host boundary, not preemption of synchronous JavaScript or sandbox confinement.\n\n`readContent(source, digest, ports, limits, signal)` invokes the supplied read callback and SHA-256 callback, checks byte bounds before copying/hashing, rejects shared buffers, and verifies an owned snapshot against the requested digest. Location carries no authority.\n\n`retainContent(digest, bytes, ports, limits, signal)` checks identity before calling the host's atomic byte-retention callback. Its acknowledgement contains only digest and byte count. It is not a signed receipt, admission or permission to reuse a response. The host must implement atomic retention and cooperate with cancellation. A callback that ignores cancellation may finish retaining bytes after the caller has refused; no success, act or authority follows from those bytes. Opening and projection never retain automatically.\n\n`invokeSigningPort(lot, sign, limits, signal)` uses the existing Topos signing request/response framing. It acquires protocol-shaped signatures; it does not decide authority or certify them cryptographically. Native export can receive `hostSigner: {name, sign}` through `/reference/native`. The name must match the admitted signer selection, and cannot be combined with a key-file override. The existing consent path verifies every returned signature against the admitted key, era, scope and epoch before returning a carrier. No key material enters the engine.\n\nNative opening accepts `content: {name, read}`. Its name must match the admitted transport selection, with that selection's existing byte and lifetime bounds. The host callback cannot be chosen by a carrier, reference or repository. The existing inspector verifies the returned carrier again and the selected backend projects only verified records. There is no backend fallback.\n\nPublic port failures use the machine refusal catalog: `host-port/budget`, `cancelled`, `timeout`, `failed`, `integrity`, `response`. Native acquisition failures retain their enclosing reference refusal. Host diagnostics are not returned as authority or parsed as codes.\n\nNative vector output, in two real admitted places:\n\n```text\nPORTS owned content · verified retention · signer content persistence failures · budget integrity cancellation timeout refused\nHOST host-sensor · declared signer and content injected · real signature verified · wrong binding refused · no commit\nHOST host-editorial · declared signer and content injected · real signature verified · wrong binding refused · no commit\n```\n" at=witness:bound021-final-bindings by=owner epoch=1 form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-host-ports shape=docs/contracts/host-ports.md sig="ed25519:era1:ZZwKOghIARrZ2O6Wz/4jjS4VMUHJYq1aFQf4y3Eums3mEYYGrxqPLQabAl3A8giq4e7sA670QPNBaiU9nUAgDw==" value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Authenticated native reference opening\n\n`@lapxo/bound/reference/native` exports `exportAuthenticatedReference` and `openAuthenticatedReference`. This is the Node host boundary. The platform-free inspector stays in `/reference`; the pure projector stays in `/engine`.\n\nThe receiver admits one `engine/object` declaration: `esm@1` or `wasm@1`. Missing, ambiguous and unknown selections refuse. WASM never falls back to ESM. The native route shares one context preparation, authenticates the received original records, then calls the existing selected projector. It introduces no algebra or alternate interpreter.\n\nThe host admits `reference/snapshot`, `reference/epoch`, `reference/proof-scope`, `reference/policy`, `reference/form-implementation`, `reference/records`, and the standing/refusal contract digests. The policy is the independently accepted export policy, not a policy invented from the received carrier. The prepared form implementation must match the admitted expectation, and both interpretation contracts must match the installed bytes. A carrier's accompanying context is public data, not an authority anchor.\n\nContent goes through the declared transport and its existing byte/time limits. The snapshot binds the declared public context, including resource budgets. The native opener accepts an AbortSignal and checks interruption before download and projection; the WASM worker uses its existing cancellation and lifetime contract. This does not claim cancellation of an in-flight native download, browser persistence or arbitrary browser capsules.\n\nWASM uses the installed asset manifest, runtime, guest and exact supported form standings. The caller cannot replace that manifest with a convenient fixture. Its memory, stack, execution, worker-lifetime and response budgets remain explicit admitted interval lines. A form outside that installed closure refuses by name.\n\nThe positive fixture uses the previously published native reader archive named in `samples/reference-backend/world-fixture.json`, or the same supported standing and closed modules already laid by digest. Preparation verifies every byte before timed opening. The fixture archive is fetched or retained outside opening measurements; no private store or checkout path identifies it. No provider code executes during archive preparation.\n\nThe native quartet opens one signed reference in two unrelated places, once through each explicitly selected backend. It compares authenticated standing, records and canonical projection bytes. Authority-context identity describes authentication, not an execution receipt: equal outputs never authorize reuse of a receipt across implementations. Neither opening signs or commits an act.\n\nCaptured semantic markers:\n\n```text\nWASM water-level-reference · authenticated download · same standing · same records · byte-equal ESM projection · altered carrier refused · no commit\nWASM editorial-reference · authenticated download · same standing · same records · byte-equal ESM projection · altered carrier refused · no commit\n```\n\nThe no sample changes the carrier while retaining its reference. Integrity refuses before projection. Existing vectors also retain unknown-backend, unsupported-form, byte-budget, authority and cancellation boundaries. The quartet descriptor is `reference-backend.publication.json`; installed-archive release acceptance and L6 remain separate obligations.\n" at=witness:bound021-final-bindings by=owner epoch=1 form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-reference-backend shape=docs/contracts/reference-backend.md sig="ed25519:era1:aH2OkVBkqfu1bGv17zLHOX7aN/Ck/3Gt4LNRxtL1ei7dU9dLsXbrNg1eHtXAVUHp1KljaeD7ruD+rCSuWXfMDw==" value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Native authenticated reference export\n\n`bound sign --as reference <source-url> --key <key-id>` exports a public snapshot from an admitted local place. The selected host signer is the place's existing signer port. File signing additionally requires its explicitly supplied `--key-file`; command signing uses the host registry. There is no implicit signer, network fetch or private export runner.\n\nThe command prints one JSON value with profile `reference-export@1`, a `reference`, the exact `carrier` text and a public `context`. It neither lands the proof nor advances the place's epoch. It does not publish, write an output directory or alter TARGET, ledgers or receipts. The caller chooses where to deliver these bytes.\n\nThe place declares `reference/authentication=authenticated-standing@1`, `reference/proof-scope`, `reference/records`, and an interval `reference/export-bytes` measured in bytes. It also pins `reference/contracts/refusals` and `reference/contracts/standing` to the exact installed contract files. Missing, conflicting or unauthenticated declarations refuse. These are place declarations, not increased general ceilings.\n\nThe exporter uses Bound's authenticated Root standing and existing context preparation. It preserves typed effective history and applies the existing configuration projection; it does not implement another fold. If the current carrier profile cannot represent historical authority, it refuses as `reference-export/history` instead of dropping originals or forging a new admission. The separate B3 quartet covers complete public standing across declared classes and committed acts; this carrier still refuses historical authority it cannot represent.\n\nThe carrier retains `authenticated-standing@1` framing. Its proof commits to the original canonical signed lines. The proof's witness snapshot also commits, through `reference-export-context@1`, to semantic standing identity, epoch, proof scope, policy, form implementation, public signers, signature algorithms, the refusal-catalog digest, the standing-contract digest, and the declared byte and record budgets. Blob identity remains separate from semantic standing identity and source location.\n\nThe exported context is public data, not an authority anchor. A consumer independently accepts its keys, policy and interpretation contracts, then supplies these expectations to `inspectAuthenticatedReference`. When `contracts` is supplied, that existing API verifies the context snapshot before requesting bytes. Altering either contract digest or a resource budget invalidates that snapshot. Existing callers without the new context retain their 0.2.0 inspection contract; that compatibility does not assert verification of the added interpretation contracts.\n\n`/engine` remains pure. Exporting a reference does not select WASM, and B1 does not claim B2's backend composition. Signature, authority and context checks precede projection. `next` recommendations from `refusals.bound` grant no permission to execute, repair or sign.\n\n## Conformance quartet\n\n- Positive data: `samples/reference-export/yes.json`. The native test bootstraps a real Root with a disposable key and admits its cell and claim through `bound land`. It then executes this public signing command and verifies the result through the public reference API and unchanged engine.\n- Negative data: `samples/reference-export/no.json`. Changing a carrier byte refuses on blob integrity before projection.\n- Vector: `vectors/native/reference-export.vector.json`. It runs the tracked conformance test, which invokes the actual CLI for bootstrap, admission and export. No private runner or pre-signed fixture stands in for those operations.\n- Additional boundaries: changed contract identities, changed byte budget, invalid source, unauthorized key and invalid signer response. Export and refusal leave the real Root's TARGET and complete ledger inventory unchanged.\n\nCaptured semantic markers from the native run:\n\n```text\nEXPORT real-admission · contracts-bound · signatures-verified · REQUIRED · no commit\nREFUSE altered-carrier · blob integrity\n```\n\nKeys are generated outside the repository and discarded by the test. Captures contain no private key, local path or successful receipt for a failed export. Fixture preparation and each CLI invocation have their own existing bounds; the enclosing conformance runner's lifetime is not a cost claim for any individual reading. Release admission, portable release receipts and exact-artifact acceptance remain separate from this quartet.\n" at=witness:bound021-final-bindings by=owner epoch=1 form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-reference-export shape=docs/contracts/reference-export.md sig="ed25519:era1:FYMGiMAR/cOWrAZ6qkSrboFOQyTIvGHOFQmmJDpi2PGCCBGhZSZlV/s/atzyZwPUQs2kvF6ZimEN0z/SNeKRCQ==" value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Public refusal catalog\n\nSource of truth: `refusals.bound` (`bound-refusals@1`), read through the existing wire parser. `readRefusalCatalog` and `describeRefusal` are exported from `/reference`; resolve `@lapxo/bound/refusals` for the published lines. The digest of these exact lines is bound into the authenticated reference context.\n\n`BoundRefusal.code` is stable; diagnostic text is not a code. Native CLI refusals retain the diagnostic and emit a `bound-refusal@1` JSON record with code, category, meaning, next and catalog digest. Recommendations grant no authority and execute nothing. If the installed catalog cannot be read or validated, the CLI emits `catalog/invalid` with metadata explicitly `unmeasured`; it never fabricates a catalog digest or recursively formats that failure. A consumer checks the catalog bytes against its independently accepted context digest before parsing; parsing alone proves no authority. Legacy operations without a specific public code use `operation/failed`; no diagnostic is classified by regex. This does not claim a distinct code for every historical diagnostic. Successful four-state results remain results, never refusal codes.\n\n| Code | Class | Meaning | Suggested next |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| reference-export/source | input | The source URL is not a canonical standing-reference location. | check-source |\n| reference-export/context | interpretation | Required admitted export policy or exact contract bytes are missing or conflicting. | inspect-standing |\n| reference-export/budget | resource | The export exceeds or lacks its explicitly declared record or byte budget. | inspect-resource-lines |\n| reference-export/authority | authority | The selected key is not authorized for the snapshot proof at this epoch. | inspect-authority |\n| reference-export/signer | host | The selected signer failed or returned a signature that does not verify. | inspect-signer |\n| reference-export/history | authority | The current carrier profile cannot represent this historical authority; no original is silently removed. | inspect-standing-contract |\n| catalog/invalid | interpretation | The refusal catalog is malformed, duplicated or incomplete. | resolve-catalog |\n| reference/format | input | The reference has an unsupported or noncanonical shape. | check-reference |\n| standing/view | interpretation | The place has not declared the public standing view. | declare-standing-view |\n| standing/query | input | Standing accepts only the local committed projection. | query-local-standing |\n| reference-export/usage | input | Reference export requires exactly one source and explicit signing options. | check-export-arguments |\n| authenticated-reference/context | interpretation | The supplied interpretation context or snapshot binding is inconsistent. | resolve-public-context |\n| authenticated-reference/budget | resource | The declared record or byte budget is invalid or exceeded. | inspect-resource-lines |\n| authenticated-reference/transport | host | The content port failed or returned shared mutable bytes. | inspect-content-port |\n| authenticated-reference/integrity | integrity | Carrier bytes or the snapshot commitment differ from their declared digest. | resolve-exact-bytes |\n| authenticated-reference/framing | input | The carrier is not the canonical supported UTF-8 frame. | check-carrier |\n| authenticated-reference/wire | input | An original record is not canonical supported wire. | check-original-records |\n| authenticated-reference/authority | authority | The proof or an original lacks admitted authority under the supplied context. | inspect-authority |\n| authenticated-reference/history | interpretation | This carrier profile cannot authenticate configuration withdrawal history. | request-admitted-standing |\n| authenticated-reference/object-context | interpretation | The existing object validator refused the declared form or context. | inspect-object-context |\n| authenticated-reference/identity | integrity | The semantic standing differs from the reference identity. | resolve-standing |\n| operation/failed | host | An operation failed without a more specific public code; details are diagnostic only. | inspect-diagnostic |\n| operation/usage | input | The command arguments do not describe a supported public operation. | check-arguments |\n| reference/backend | interpretation | The host did not select a supported explicit reference backend. | select-reference-backend |\n| reference/cancelled | host | The opening was interrupted before a complete result. | inspect-interruption |\n| host-port/budget | resource | A host port lacks a valid finite budget or exceeds its byte bound. | inspect-resource-lines |\n| host-port/cancelled | host | A host operation was cancelled before a complete result. | inspect-interruption |\n| host-port/timeout | host | A host operation exceeded its declared lifetime. | inspect-host-port |\n| host-port/failed | host | A declared host callback failed; no successful result is returned. | inspect-host-port |\n| host-port/integrity | integrity | Content bytes differ from the requested SHA-256 identity. | resolve-exact-bytes |\n| host-port/response | host | A host response has an unsupported shape or shared mutable bytes. | inspect-host-port |\n\nNative quartet: `refusals.publication.json`. `vectors/native/refusals.vector.json` checks the wire catalog, its derived page, duplicate/missing records, and real export/inspection refusals in two places. No failed operation becomes a successful receipt. The captured semantic marker is:\n\n```text\nREFUSALS lines · unique codes · exact metadata · duplicate refused\n```\n\nSpecific producers cover reference syntax, standing query/view, native export usage/source/context/budget/authority/signer/history, and authenticated inspection context/budget/transport/integrity/framing/wire/authority/history/object-context/identity. Numeric CLI usage and uncoded legacy failures use the explicitly generic codes above; diagnostics remain unclassified. B2/B5 may add codes only by changing these versioned contract bytes and their vectors.\n" at=witness:bound021-final-bindings by=owner epoch=1 form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-refusals shape=docs/contracts/refusals.md sig="ed25519:era1:1TZmFIFYbegwARYyMezeUtQ5sVyFntNjNRgDfWwxYgLxLHL47/yXqlk2IttFl1njWTwk/9crWnP/DTgU89bSBA==" value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Complete public standing\n\nSource of truth: `standing.bound`. Resolve `@lapxo/bound/standing-contract` for these exact contract lines. The authenticated export context binds their digest beside the refusal catalog digest.\n\n`bound fold --as standing` reads admitted evidence across every declared key class and committed local acts. Authentication uses the grant and signature vocabulary at each admission epoch. Storage layout is not authority. No consumer reconstructs journals or chooses the first declaration on collision.\n\n- **standing/classes: all-declared.** Standing is authenticated under admission snapshots across declared key classes.\n- **standing/duplicates: preserve-attribution.** Exact redelivery does not establish another origin.\n- **standing/withdrawals: exact.** Exact configuration withdrawals are applied before publication; typed records retain their admitted effective history.\n- **standing/conflicts: preserve.** Incompatible admitted declarations are preserved; no first-wins selection.\n- **standing/history: distinct.** Historical receipts are evidence, not current authority or a current response.\n- **standing/publication: live-configuration.** Public current configuration omits withdrawal markers; authenticated historical records remain available through evidence and act views.\n- **standing/namespaces: preserve.** The contextual carrier separates Root, legacy places and committed place contexts; clocks are never flattened.\n- **standing/effective-order: admitted.** Typed records retain admitted effective order; standing is evidence rather than a new grant of authority.\n\nThe legacy Root output is canonical signed lines. A place declaring `place-act-context@1` returns its existing JSON carrier: `root`, `legacyPlaces`, `places`, and authenticated `contexts`. Namespaces and independent clocks remain separate; contextual data never grants Root authority. Configuration revisions and exact withdrawals have already been applied. Typed effective history is passed to the existing object projection, not treated as configuration. `standing` is current evidence; `evidence` and `act` preserve history.\n\nNative quartet: `standing.publication.json`. The same real CLI vector runs in observations and inventory, in journal and committed-act modes. It prints:\n\n```text\nSTANDING observations journal · all classes · collisions retained · exact withdrawals · replay identical · forged excluded\n```\n\nThe no sample changes admitted bytes without resigning: the forged row is excluded while valid rows remain. Later withdrawal of a key does not erase its historically admitted evidence. No private fixture key, path or run log ships with the quartet.\n" at=witness:bound021-final-bindings by=owner epoch=1 form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-standing shape=docs/contracts/standing.md sig="ed25519:era1:HOSPLM+kYsp5hJsboo+D1EjRNxEbxtC2wROfIQgDBBoA6gOGJ/xLdmr18uaITw32mjuEr6Jfgx+7wL90EWnpAg==" value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="## Typed cell, claim and mark records\n\nThe object profile is selected by admitted `wire/object/*` claims in a historical wire snapshot. The epoch of `wire/object/types` is its activation E; a typed record must have epoch >= E. An older record keeps the older grammar. Merely adding object words to `wire/fields` does not admit a typed profile.\n\n`fromLine` selects grammar before validating required fields: a record without `type` remains configuration, retaining its required `at`, `role`, `form`, `measure` and `value`; a typed record selects its exact required/allowed row. Configuration cannot carry object-only fields. An incomplete typed record receives no configuration defaults. The parse result distinguishes configuration from cell/claim/mark. `sign=+1|-1` is an object act, `sig` its signature envelope, and a host sign operation produces that envelope.\n\nThe common object envelope is `type`, `scope`, `id`, `epoch`, `by`, `sig`. Object records reject `at`: epoch orders acts, and `widens` names a local join witness. The following is the declared reference profile. The admitted row alphabets, not this table or a host decoder, are the source of required/allowed field permission.\n\n| Row | Fields in addition to the common envelope | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| cell | form, measure, params, restsOn, topos | Define a cell and its selected form/context. |\n| claim/positive | sign, origin, value | Observe an encoded span, sign=+1. |\n| claim/negative | sign, takes | Withdraw an exact claim ID, sign=-1. |\n| mark/travelling | sign, pole, reach, value | Ceiling sign: sign=+1, pole=ceiling, reach=travels. |\n| mark/local | sign, pole, reach, value, widens | Local join: sign=+1, pole=ceiling, reach=local; another mark, retaining the travelling sign. |\n| mark/floor-travelling | sign, pole, reach, value | Travelling require: sign=+1, pole=floor, reach=travels; no widens. |\n| mark/negative | sign, takes | Withdraw an exact mark ID, sign=-1; no pole/reach/widens. |\n\nEach row has both `wire/object/required/<row>` and `wire/object/allowed/<row>`. Unknown keys, missing required keys, floor/local, floor+widens, and travelling widening refuse without coercion. A configuration demand is unrelated to a floor mark. `by` authenticates a signing key; it is not an independent observation origin.\n\nFor a cell, `restsOn=none` represents no parents; otherwise it is a canonical byte-ordered alphabet of coordinates. Several parents are permitted. Only declared cell-coordinate edges form object rest; a directory prefix, a configuration artifact digest, and a host source location do not. Context validation rejects reachable cycles before algebraic parts are requested.\n\nThe cell's `topos` names folded standing, and its `params` identifies declared parameter bytes. A selected provider decodes values and supplies origin/witness authorization from admitted evidence. A host does not infer interval endpoints, map a key to an origin, or accept widens as its own witness. Missing pin, artifact, decoder, origin or witness refuses by cause. Cell definitions and pins are immutable in this profile; migration is not inferred.\n\n`objectHistory` preserves typed history separately from configuration; `validateObjectContext` checks IDs, cell ownership, exact takes, witness order and declared rest under the selected context. Exact redelivery is idempotent; one ID with different canonical content refuses. Signature-envelope differences never bypass verification.\n\nAfter admission, the instrument projects the acts into Obligations. Sign and require travel along declared rest; a witnessed join is local. Descendants inherit travelling marks without local widening. Live exact-ID withdrawals remain confined to their authenticated history. Keys, snapshots, signing devices and repeated receipts do not create independent origins. Compatible meets are not forks. Object states and their precedence come from Obligations; configuration folds do not compute object encounters.\n" at=witness:minimal-contract-015 by=owner epoch=1 form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-typed-object shape=docs/contracts/typed-object.md sig="ed25519:era1:+s3VzmoXCNltl43B84cdkbU9zoUJJr/RpOGhu5YtlJqkzhZy4lszt+WJBwX8sPt8xLvTRnmn9e0z+3DzZl3eBg==" value=lock
bound-lock/1 about="# Help view\n\nA declared help view projects the instrument verb and flag table. It is generated from that table, not copied prose or a claim that another world is supported. Asking it does not authorize a line or call a selected external reader. The sample declares view/help value=help, checks the printed three verbs and refuses a declared view whose region has no provider.\n\n# Lines view\n\nA declared lines view projects canonical normative standing as wire lines. It is a configuration reading; it does not compute encounters or turn evidence into authority. Live exact withdrawals disappear from normative standing while authenticated evidence remains inspectable through its own view. The sample verifies signatures and attribution, redelivery ONCE, exact withdrawals, tampering and uncovered writes. A declared missing provider refuses rather than returning an invented reading.\n\n# Receipts view\n\nThe native receipt view projects the fold through the admitted Topos receipt contract. The sample uses the published place profile receipts@8|receipts@0|receipts@1: observations, summary and current region bytes. These are that profile’s declared projections, not a universal enumeration of resolutions. A receipt identifies the actual observed set; it is not an origin or a valuation. The sample obtains a native receipt, verifies its signed delivery from an attest-class key through evidence, and checks idempotent delivery. The refused sample names an uncovered write, tampered signature and declared view without a provider. No harness-written JSON is used as a receipt.\n\n## Public verification\n\nFull-resolution native reading receipts carry the verified canonical UTF-8 input closure in the bytes row and the verified output in the count row, through the wire’s existing about field. Count, digest and measured duration keep their existing meanings. The host verifies the supplied bytes against those digests before reuse; an altered supplied carrier is not replaced by a cached copy. Historical local-only receipts still require their authentic CAS bytes. No private ledger or store is needed to verify a complete public receipt.\n\nFile-region seals hash configuration through Topos’s existing publication projection. Admission envelopes and retired history stay in the authority ledger; live declarations and ordinary file bytes remain inputs. Object history uses its own contract. A public checkout must carry receipts made for its actual files and compiled reader, not seals copied from a different tree. Verification does not run readers, fetch dependencies, refresh receipts or grant authority.\n\n# Why a coordinate\n\n`bound fold <coordinate> --as why` explains the live declarations whose exact `needs` member or `shape` names that coordinate. It prints the selector, witness and value. It does not compute an object encounter or infer authority from a path. The published sample bootstraps two independent places, explains each declared source coordinate and prints `OPEN` for a coordinate no declaration names. That absence is not zero, green or a state. A declared view with no provider refuses by name with a nonzero status. Calling why without a coordinate is not this sample’s acceptance vector; its empty historical capture pays nothing.\n\n# Declared programs\n\nThe programs view asks the selected Topos demand-status projection at resolution 8. Selection, minimum, evidence, elapsed reading and time ceiling coordinates belong to that Topos contract; Bound only hands admitted declarations and region receipts. Program status is derived from that evidence and the declared dependency graph. A demand with no evidence remains unread, its dependant remains blocked, and a missing elapsed reading keeps deadline unread. No cell encounter, origin count or information quantity is computed by this view. The sample uses the published SDK implementation on two unrelated demand chains, captures equal readings twice, names the next unblocked coordinate, and refuses dependency cycles. It does not provide stage conformance evidence or claim either program closed.\n\n# Demand card\n\nThe declared card view asks the selected Topos demand-card projection at resolution 8. It reads the selected demand coordinates, their explicit dependencies and the handed evidence region under selectors declared by that Topos. It does not infer progress from files or convert a declaration into proof. A requirement without evidence is unread; an unpaid predecessor blocks its dependant. The next coordinate is an unblocked unpaid demand. Deadline is unread when no elapsed receipt is handed; absence is not zero. These labels describe program evidence, not Obligations cell states. The sample pins the implementation member from the published Topos SDK, folds two unrelated places twice with equal stdout and refuses a declared dependency cycle. This sample intentionally has no evidence to close either program; it certifies the projection of absence and order, not stage acceptance.\n" at=witness:public-contracts-by-operation by=owner epoch=1 form=alphabet measure=text role=writes scope=prose/en/files/contract-views shape=docs/contracts/views.md sig="ed25519:era1:x7vXoNMfI4cTVdZPxoU50IBuPYRcJ+Q5qSsASBnEnSCTh+a4NtU/EdS8VEwbKN8Xax6PtC2GHs6s1ZFz9mEgBw==" value=lock
